window.docContents[244].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___10___-Managed-Entities":{"contents":"It is the responsibility of the persistence provider to ensure that at most one entity instance belonging to a given persistence context has a given persistent identity. The persistent identity of an entity class instance is represented as a pair comprising:\n\n1. the class of the root entity of the entity class inheritance hierarchy to which the entity class belongs, together with2. the value of the primary key of the entity instance, as defined above in Section 2.4.\n\nTherefore, if the entity type X is a subclass of the root entity type Y, and x is an instance of X with primary key k, the persistent identity of x is (Y,k).\n\nIt is the responsibility of the application to ensure that an instance of an entity class is associated with at most one persistence context. If an entity instance is made managed in more than one persistence context, the behavior is undefined and unportable.\n\nThe contains() method of EntityManager determines whether an entity instance is managed in the associated persistence context. The contains() method returns true if and only if:\n\n* the argument is a managed entity instance associated with the given persistence context, and* the argument entity is not marked as removed.\n\nThe effect of cascading persist, merge, remove, or detach is immediately visible to the contains() method, whereas the actual insertion, update, or deletion of the database record represented by the entity is deferred until the flush operation occurs.\n\n\n\nFormalized SpecificationWe now state the conditions that the model must\npreserve. An invariant is a condition we prove true initially and after\nevery allowed operation. Its name in square brackets identifies the condition\nin later proofs.First, an instance is new if and only if it has no persistent identity.\nThe symbol ↔ means “if and only if,” so the condition applies in both\ndirections. The uppercase variable E makes this statement apply to every\nentity value.invariant [identityOK] (e_phase E = new ↔ pid E = noIdentity)\nAn instance that has not been constructed must retain its initial lifecycle\nstate and identity. Once an instance has been created, this condition places\nno further restriction on it.invariant [uncreatedOK] ¬ created E → e_phase E = new ∧ pid E = noIdentity\nFinally, two instances in the context with the same persistent identity must\nbe the same Java object. We declare this as a safety property: a condition\nthat must hold in every reachable state. Here E and OTHER range\nover every pair of instances.safety [uniqueIdentity] belongsToContext E ∧ belongsToContext OTHER ∧ pid E = pid OTHER → E = OTHER\nBecause belongsToContext includes managed and removed instances,\nuniqueness applies to both. Detached instances are outside the context and may\nshare an identity with a managed copy.We prove that the initial state satisfies all three conditions, then show that\neach operation preserves them whenever they hold before the operation.The command #gen_spec turns our initialization, actions, and properties\ninto Lean definitions used by the proofs.#gen_spec\nInitially, every instance is new and has noIdentity, so the identity\ncondition holds. The command prove_veil_invariant_goal identifies the\ninitialization or action and the property to prove. The phrase using tr\nselects its transition relation: the definition of the allowed before-and-after\nstates. After by, we give Lean the proof instructions.The tactic grind automatically combines the available facts to construct\na proof. Lean's kernel checks that proof; we do not accept a solver's answer\nwithout a proof.prove_veil_invariant_goal initializer identityOK using tr by\n  grind\nInitialization also gives every uncreated instance its required defaults.prove_veil_invariant_goal initializer uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\nNo instance belongs to the context initially, so two context members cannot\nhave conflicting identities.prove_veil_invariant_goal initializer uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  grind\nAn unconstructed instance starts in phase new with noIdentity,\nand Create changes only its construction flag. Since only managed and\nremoved instances belong to the context, both assertions in Create hold.\nThe doesNotThrow proof checks this for every construction from a state\nsatisfying the model's invariants. The using wp form works backward from\nthe assertions to the conditions before construction.prove_veil_invariant_goal Create doesNotThrow using wp by\n  have hd := phase_EnumClass.distinct (phase := phase)\n  simp [distinctN_cons] at hd\n  grind\nCreation also preserves the three invariants. The new instance is outside the\ncontext, so it cannot duplicate the identity of a context member.The uniqueness proof explicitly uses the fact that the four lifecycle states\nare distinct. The command have names that fact hd, and\nsimp rewrites it into facts that grind can use.prove_veil_invariant_goal Create identityOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Create uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Create uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  have hd := phase_EnumClass.distinct (phase := phase)\n  simp [distinctN_cons] at hd\n  grind\nPersist gives a new instance an actual identity when making it managed, or\nmakes a removed instance managed without changing its identity. It never\nmodifies an uncreated instance. The identityAvailable condition ensures that an\nidentity assigned to a new instance cannot collide with one in the context.prove_veil_invariant_goal Persist identityOK using tr by\n  have hd := phase_EnumClass.distinct (phase := phase)\n  simp [distinctN_cons] at hd\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Persist uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Persist uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  grind\nRemove changes managed instances to removed. Both states require a persistent\nidentity and belong to the context, so the identity and uniqueness conditions\ncontinue to hold. Uncreated instances are untouched.prove_veil_invariant_goal Remove identityOK using tr by\n  have hd := phase_EnumClass.distinct (phase := phase)\n  simp [distinctN_cons] at hd\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Remove uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Remove uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  grind\nRefresh makes no assignments. Each of the three conditions therefore remains\ntrue for exactly the same state as before the call.prove_veil_invariant_goal Refresh identityOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Refresh uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Refresh uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  grind\nDetach preserves identity while changing managed or removed instances to\ndetached. These states all have persistent identities. Removing an instance\nfrom the context cannot introduce an identity conflict, and the action leaves\nuncreated instances untouched.prove_veil_invariant_goal Detach identityOK using tr by\n  have hd := phase_EnumClass.distinct (phase := phase)\n  simp [distinctN_cons] at hd\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Detach uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Detach uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  grind\nMerge either returns an existing managed instance or creates a managed target.\nA new target receives an actual identity. For a new source, merge assigns an\navailable identity to the target. For a detached source, creation requires that\nthe source's identity is absent from the context. These conditions preserve\nuniqueness, while all other uncreated instances retain their defaults.prove_veil_invariant_goal Merge identityOK using tr by\n  have hd := phase_EnumClass.distinct (phase := phase)\n  simp [distinctN_cons] at hd\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Merge uncreatedOK using tr by\n  grind\n\nprove_veil_invariant_goal Merge uniqueIdentity using tr by\n  grind\nWith the individual proofs supplied, #check_invariants checks that the\ninvariant obligations have been discharged.#check_invariants\nThe command #gen_theorems assembles the checked results into theorems\nthat the execution proofs in the next chapter can use.#gen_theorems\nThe command #gen_executable generates executable versions of the same\nactions for finite model checks.#gen_executable\nWe can now explore a small instance of the model. The Lean type Fin 3\ncontains the values 0, 1, and 2. We use these for three possible Java objects\nand three identity values. Reserving identity 0 for noIdentity leaves\ntwo actual persistent identities.The command #model_check interpreted explores the reachable states of\nthis finite instance and checks the model's properties. This is an executable\ncheck for these bounds; the proofs above apply without those bounds.#model_check interpreted { entity := Fin 3, identity := Fin 3 } { noIdentity := 0 }\nThis completes the lifecycle module.end EntityLifecycle\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations\u00093.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle","header":"3.6.10. Managed Entities","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___10___-Managed-Entities"}});